      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/baseline-samples/17592186054676/assets/5c4455134b48a394731e78eb381e889f3f9320be92e0c9615598b921b30ea5bf.png)\n\nWhich of the given points lie on the line $y=-2x-2$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(0,-2)$, $B(-4,7)$, $C(-1,3)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(0,-2)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x-2 \\\\ (-2) &= -2(0)-2 \\\\ -2 &= -2 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(-4,7)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x-2 \\\\ (7) &= -2(-4)-2 \\\\ 7 &= 6 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(-1,3)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x-2 \\\\ (3) &= -2(-1)-2 \\\\ 3 &= 0 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does not lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $A$ lies on the line.",
